command go run ./cmd/loadgen -embed -label acceptance7 -hosts 4000 -ttl 10 -renew 3s -kill-after 2s -keep 20s -register-timeout 180s

previous keepalive_ok of exactly 28000
The previous loadgen did not call registrar. After each host's hand-written redial it started a 3s ticker. The first tick is one period later. The process then slept 2s + 20s = 22s after the slowest redial. Seven ticks need 21s and eight need 24s, so a host whose ticker started within 3s of the slowest finish records exactly 7 successes. Those redials shared 64 clients and finished in a cluster (registered at 4008ms, all redials done at 7387ms), which is why the total was exactly 7*4000. The txn compared LeaseValue, not ModRevision. That count was not registrar's renew loop.

this run
Each host is a registrar. It calls Drain, then Ready with a new egress. Renewals are the registrar's own KeepAliveOnce loop, counted from the ka_ok event. keepalive_ok is 41903, per host min 10 / p50 10 / max 11. The start stagger is 8s, about two renew intervals, which is the 10 vs 11 split. It is not a fixed multiple of 4000.
Drain holds the registrar lock, so 4000 simultaneous Drain calls stall keepalive and the 10s TTL expires. An unbounded attempt lost 3999 leases (drain_err 4000). This run caps Drain+Ready at 64 in flight (-redial-inflight). That cap is only in the load generator.
keepalive_err is 6, sample "etcdserver: server stopped", from killing the leader. lease_lost is 0. watch_del equals the 4000 planned drains. watch_put is 8000: one create plus one put after Ready.

